(Phnom Penh): Ms. Yang Sarim is a Cambodian citizen who survived the Khmer Rouge regime. To this day, despite being overthrown for 40 years, the 75-year-old still remembers all the tragedy and suffering she suffered.
She recalled that during this regime, you lost your husband and three children, as well as some relatives. Ms. Yang Sarim, who currently lives in Phnom Penh's 7 Makara district, also recalled many other hardships in her life as a widow struggling to raise her children after the fall of the Khmer Rouge regime.
The Pol Pot genocidal regime of 3 years, 8 months and 20 days is one of the most brutal regimes in Khmer history. More than 2 million Cambodians have died from homicide, torture, displacement, and disease.
To commemorate the atrocities of the Khmer leaders in Democratic Kampuchea and the suffering of the people in that regime, the Royal Government of Cambodia has organized a National Day of Remembrance on May 20 every year since 1983. Some people consider the celebration of this day to be nonsense.
But for Ms. Yang Sarim, she did not understand like some people. She considers this day as an important day for the nation, not only to remind the Cambodian people of the atrocities in the Democratic Kampuchea regime, but also to remind the leaders and the Cambodian people together to protect Achieve national peace, do not let the nation fall into destruction like in the past.
