Legal Awareness: Reasons for Criminal Liability
(Phnom Penh): When another person commits an act of aggression against another person, the law stipulates that the act is a criminal offense, the perpetrator will be punished according to the act he committed, but there is still a reason Some that the Penal Code does not require liability or alleviate criminal liability.
To better understand what are the possible reasons for avoiding criminal liability or easing criminal liability? Fresh News Legal Awareness Program in collaboration with Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation would like to present the following:
Pursuant to the Criminal Code of the Kingdom of Cambodia 2009 stipulates the reasons for non-criminal liability or mitigation of criminal liability as follows:
1. Non-criminal liability due to dementia (Article 31 of the Penal Code) For a limited period of time or for a short period of time, therefore, if a person commits an offense when that person has a mental disorder that results in the loss of his or her cognitive ability, he or she shall not be criminally liable.
On the other hand, if a person develops a mental disorder that only reduces his or her sense of guilt, that person is still criminally liable. Determining whether a person has a mental disorder or simply diminishes their sense of responsibility requires clear evidence from a specialist for the court to examine and consider the matter in determining the sentence.
For a person who suffers from dementia due to the use of alcohol, drugs or substances that are prohibited by law and has committed a crime, he can not be released from criminal liability.
But conspirators, instigators of genocide, crimes against humanity or war crimes cannot be exempted from criminal responsibility for any reason.
3. Legal Protection (Article 33 of the Penal Code) The law allows any person who commits an offense in a state of legal protection not to be criminally liable, but that legal protection must have the following conditions:
In this case, Mr. "B" is not criminally responsible, because this act is a necessary condition to protect others who respond to the aggression at the same time of the act, and the means of self-defense used in proportion.
4. The Reality of Legal Protection (Article 34 of the Penal Code) The presumption of legal protection in the event that an offense is committed to forcing a night entry due to violent or manipulative burglary. In places where there are people.
The offense was committed to protect oneself against theft or violent robbery. The reality of legal protection is not absolute. This fact can be distorted by the opposite evidence.
5. Necessary situation (Article 35 of the Penal Code) Necessary situation refers to a person who is in a state of necessity to commit an offense and is not criminally liable. Locations need to have the following conditions:
The offense is deemed justified by the need to defend oneself, protect others or protect property against an imminent or imminent danger, and there is no disproportion between the means of protection and the severity of the danger.
6. The force of force or coercion (Article 36 of the Penal Code) refers to a person who commits an offense under the force of force or coercion that this person can not resist is not criminally liable. Force and coercion can only come from events beyond human will. This force and coercion must be unpredictable and unavoidable.
In summary, the Penal Code specifically provides for the causes of irresponsibility, as well as the provision of criminal liability, in order to protect individuals who have lost consciousness while committing crimes under the auspices of Legal and self-defense authorities protect others or protect property in the face of illegal encroachment.
In addition to the above reasons, minors under the age of 14 shall not be subject to criminal penalties, but acts committed by minors, parents or guardians for minors are liable for civil damages.
