Cambodia sends nearly 300 Blue Hat troops to Mali mission
(Phnom Penh): 298 Cambodian Blue Hat troops, 25 women, the Airport Engineering Force No. 891 and the Unexploded Ordnance Squadron No. 231 have been sent on a UN-flagged peacekeeping mission in the Republic of Malina. This afternoon, May 17, 2019.
The handover ceremony of the Cambodian Blue Hat Army was presided over by General Pol Saroeun, Senior Minister in charge of sending Cambodian forces to the United Nations peacekeeping mission on behalf of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia.
General Sem Sovanny, Director General of the National Center for Peacekeeping Forces and Demining and Explosive Ordnance Disposal, said that the accompanying forces had received quality vocational training from trainers. Cambodia and UNMAS officials for effective participation, as in previous divisions, and this force comes from units under the Ministry of National Defense, the National Gendarmerie Command and the National Center.
On that occasion, Senior Minister Pol Saroeun stated that the Royal Government of Cambodia is firmly committed to the sustainable goal of sending its security forces to continue to participate in the UN mission to continue to contribute to the international community in the cause of peace in Cambodia. In the world.
In addition, the Royal Government of Cambodia has encouraged the operation of peacekeeping operations and has already prepared forces in the rapid reserve system to provide support to the actual capabilities and capabilities as requested by the United Nations and is committed to promoting Use female forces to ensure the continuity of participation in long-term peacekeeping missions.
According to Senior Minister Pol Saroeun, for the past 13 years, Cambodia has been sending thousands of Blue Hat troops to peacekeeping missions under the United Nations umbrella, but peacekeeping is facing many challenges. Yes, the state has waged a war of uprisings against Cambodian peacekeepers, resulting in many casualties.
Senior Minister Pol Saroeun also thanked the presence of the United Nations Resident Coordinator in Cambodia, who was always present to support the Cambodian Peacekeeping Force. United Nations Humanitarian Mission.
According to the report, since 2006, Cambodia has sent 6,053 Blue Hat troops to 285 countries, including 285 women, to conduct peacekeeping missions in various countries. Cambodia has become the largest contributor to ASEAN, with peacekeepers on mission in other countries. Currently, 797 Cambodian troops, 54 of them women, are deployed in some countries in Africa and the Middle East.
