China stops large-scale purchase of pork from US, costs US more than $ 6.5 billion
WASHINGTON (Reuters) - In the same week that President Donald Trump announced an increase in tariffs on Chinese imports, Beijing canceled more than 1,247 tons of pork orders from the United States, according to the US Department of Agriculture. This is the largest order cancellation in more than a year. This was released by Reuters on Friday evening, May 17, 2019.
According to the US Department of Agriculture, the cancellation occurred on May 9 and cost the US pork export market more than $ 6.5 billion. Mainland China and Hong Kong are the second largest export destinations for US pork. But the sector is beginning to suffer after the two countries went to war, raising tariffs on each other, and especially after the outbreak of African swine flu.
