The Prime Minister of Nepal's first visit to Cambodia is from 13 to 15 May 2019. After arriving in Cambodia in the afternoon of the same day, Sama Oli will lay a wreath at the Independence Monument and the coffin of King Norodom Sihanouk.
Afterwards, the Prime Minister of Nepal will visit the Peace Palace, where Samdech Techo Hun Sen will receive and officially receive the visit. After meeting and discussing some important work, in the evening, Samdech Techo Hun Sen will organize a dinner for the Prime Minister of Nepal and the delegation.
On May 14, Mr. Sama Oli will pay a courtesy call on Samdech Vibol Sena Pheakdey Say Chhum, President of the Senate, and Samdech Akka Moha Ponhea Chakrei Heng Samrin, President of the National Assembly. In the afternoon, Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Mr. Sama Oli presided over the Cambodia-Nepal Business Forum. The two leaders will also deliver keynote speeches at this important forum.
During the visit, the Prime Minister of Nepal will also visit historical sites and other important places in Phnom Penh.
Although relations have been re-established for almost 20 years, Cambodia does not currently have an embassy in Nepal (based in New Delhi, India) and Nepal does not have an embassy in Cambodia. (Residence in Bangkok, Thailand).
In December 2018, Samdech Techo Hun Sen led a Cambodian delegation on an official visit and attend the Asia-Pacific Summit in Nepal.
In terms of culture, the Government of Nepal, the birthplace of the Buddha, allowed Cambodians to build a pagoda called "Lampini" in 2006, and in February 2018, Hun Neng, chairman of the National Assembly, Interior, National Defense and Public Works Committee, and The family presided over the groundbreaking ceremony for the Preah Vihear district and the inauguration of various achievements at the Khmer Lampini pagoda.
Trade relations between the two countries are still small. According to a report by the Ministry of Commerce, from 2014 to the first six months of 2018, the trade volume between the two countries was nearly 170,000 US dollars. Of that, Cambodia exported to Nepal more than 120,000 dollars and Cambodia imported from Nepal more than 40,000 dollars. Most of the goods traded are garments and raw materials for garment production.
Nepal covers an area of 147,181 square kilometers, borders India and China, and has a total population of over 29 million, of which more than 81% are Hindus, 9% Buddhists. More than 4 percent are Muslim and more than 5 percent are of other religions.
