(Battambang): Dr. Hang Chuon Naron, Minister of Education, Youth and Sports, announces the appointment of Mr. Yi Songky as the new Director of the Department of Education, Youth and Sports in Battambang Province to replace Mr. Ngy Set, who has retired.
The appointment ceremony was held on May 10, 2019 at the Battambang Provincial Hall with the participation of Mr. Soeum Bunrith, Deputy Governor, Mr. Uth Ry, Chairman of the Provincial Council, Representative of the Pailin Provincial Department of Education, Banteay Meanchey Province, Governor of Pursat. Municipalities, districts, department heads, unit directors, deputy principals, secondary schools, high schools, teachers, government officials around the province as well.
Mr. Ngy Set, former director of the Department of Education, said that the education sector in Battambang province has grown significantly at present and the commitment of Mr. Yi Songky, director of the Provincial Department of Education, Youth and Sports, has just been appointed to the new position and the appointment announcement has been made. And handed over the seal by Dr. Hang Chuon Naron.
On that occasion, Mr. Soeum Bunrith thanked the former director of the department for his excellent work on education and left many achievements for the next generation to continue their work and congratulate the director. The new department to carry out shift work to the future with high efficiency.
"As the Department of Education, we need to strengthen the management committees in schools, teachers and students at all levels, and encourage students to come to school regularly so that our students can gain knowledge," he said. Study until the end of high school so that students have the opportunity to continue their studies in other majors at the university.
He added that at the pre-school level, we also have a policy to strengthen the capacity of students from the lower level and expand more schools in rural areas in the near future to serve the needs of students in both urban and rural areas.
He added that on behalf of the Department of Education and all education staff must cooperate with the authorities and local authorities to check in the clubs, gambling places, drug use must be prevented to avoid students addicted to play and use not to go to school. And must educate students to respect traffic laws as well.
This is a good reason to expand and increase human resources from the grassroots to the most important, because the younger generation is the successor of bamboo, continue to build and develop the country in the future. Afterwards, the Minister decorated the former director of the department.
Another new microfinance institution jumps into the Cambodian stock market
(Phnom Penh): Friday, the 7th day of the month of Pisak in the year of Kor Eksak, B.C. 2562 On May 10, 2019, the Cambodia Securities Exchange (CSX) has officially registered the sale of LOLC's bonds. The ceremony was presided over by Dr. Hean Sahip, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Economy and Finance and Chairman of the Board of Directors of the Cambodia Securities Exchange.
The event was also attended by the Deputy Governor of the National Bank of Cambodia, the Director General of the Securities and Exchange Commission of Cambodia, the Director General of the Securities and Exchange Commission of Cambodia, the Chief Executive Officer of LOLC Microfinance Institution, Mr. - Director of Yanta Securities and related members, as well as all management and staff of LOLC Microfinance Institution.
This bond is valid for 3 years from April 26, 2019 to April 26, 2022. The first feature of the bond is not only to provide a high and stable coupon rate to the bondholders, but also to provide hedging the exchange rate risk that may occur in the future. The first corporate card that incorporates such features listed on the CSX.
He said the issuance of bonds was a new diversification of the Cambodia Securities Exchange and a new source of finance in addition to the existing ones. This result has provided many benefits for Cambodia, such as reducing operating costs for additional financial mobilization, more efficient use of capital, building corporate governance, and operational accountability.
(Phnom Penh): The Japanese Ambassador to Cambodia, Hidehisa Horinochi, revealed that as the sun heats up, people in Japan often put up "green curtains" in their homes, gardens or buildings to resist the heat. Light and intense heat emission from the outside, as well as to provide a green aesthetic.
The Japanese ambassador explained that the "green curtain" is the planting of green vines near windows or glass walls to reduce the light and heat from the sun that enters people's homes and office buildings.
Speaking in Khmer in Japanese accent, the Ambassador also called on Cambodians to try this green curtain, as the Japanese people prefer, because Cambodia is rich in green vines, which are very useful. .
