2: How does the election of the Prime Minister work?
To elect a new prime minister, the Thai parliament, which includes the lower house and the upper house, must hold a joint vote. However, the 250-seat Senate, which is directly elected by the military government, is likely to support the PPRP, which has incumbent Prime Minister Prayuth Chan Ocha as its prime ministerial candidate.
For the above reasons, Mr. Prayuth Chan Ocha is considered by analysts to remain the head of government, but as the elected Prime Minister, not a coup d'etat. To run for prime minister, each candidate needs 376 votes. Following the release of the official results, the prime ministerial election is expected to take place in a few weeks.
3: Why is the release of official results delayed so long?
The first general election since the 2014 military coup did not go smoothly. The Thai Election Commission (NEC) has been widely criticized for alleged irregularities in the election process, with official results released just days after the election. Will be postponed until May.
