(Phnom Penh): The Minister of Tourism, Mr. Thong Khon, criticized the officials who disseminated legal documents to Chinese business owners for not translating those documents into Chinese and instructed all relevant tourism departments to take High attention to Chinese business owners, especially to call them to disseminate laws, regulations, by translating or explaining in Chinese.
Such instructions by Minister Thong Khon were made during the first plenary session of the sixth mandate of the Inter-Ministerial Commission to facilitate the travel and transportation of tourists and passengers at the international border crossings and ports. International doors on May 08, 2019 at the Ministry of Tourism.
The minister said that in the past, there have been frequent meetings to disseminate legal documents and regulations to tourism business owners, but some gaps have also occurred, such as less invitation to ethnic business owners. Many Chinese have come and gone, but there is no translation of documents into Chinese or no explanation in Chinese.
He added that some Chinese do not know English and even do not know Khmer, without Chinese translation or explanation to them is not effective in enforcing the law, so all tourism departments and relevant departments All need to rethink this issue.
"Foreign businessmen do not want to oppose the government or violate the laws of our country. They want to listen and do what is right, but we did not tell them all because we did not want to," he said. "Translated into Chinese, called Chinese to the meeting, there is no translator, and all legal documents are legal in Khmer, but Chinese is not available, so there must be a Chinese translation distributed to them."
The Minister of Tourism also raised the issue of store signage, urging all shop owners or companies in Cambodia to put Khmer letters at the top, English letters in the middle but smaller. The upper half of the Khmer script, while the lower part of the business owner can put the third country letters, such as Chinese, Japanese, Korean, Thai, French and so on.
At this point, he said that Chinese shops can put Chinese characters in the middle, but must put Khmer characters at the top and must be larger than half of the Chinese characters, that is, can not avoid using English because Cambodia is a multi-ethnic tourist destination, not just Chinese tourists. According to Mr. Thong Khon, this practice is also being adjusted gradually remarkably.
