Cambodia needs investors and development partners to help develop new tourism products and infrastructure to support tourism
(Phnom Penh): Cambodia needs more investors and development partners to help develop new tourism products and infrastructure to support tourism and to improve service quality in line with new travel trends and to expand direct flight connections. . This was stated by the Secretary of State of the Ministry of Tourism, Mr. Hor Moniroth.
Mr. Hor Moniroth said this on the occasion of presiding over the cultural and tourism promotion event at the Royal Fall Royal Hotel on the afternoon of May 8, 2019.
Mr. Hor Moniroth said that the friendship and solidarity between the two countries have been continuously strengthened and expanded since the establishment of diplomatic relations in 1958 to the present and in the future with mutual respect and trust. Based on strategic partners from all angles. This relationship has led to the building of a common destiny community between Cambodia and China.
The Secretary of State stated that Cambodia under the win-win policy of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister, has brought peace, stability and development in all areas, including tourism, which is considered as green gold. In the last decade, in 2018, tourism brought in $ 4.375 billion and contributed 12.7% of gross domestic product (GDP).
According to Cambodian tourism statistics, in 2018, Cambodia received more than 6.2 million international tourists, an increase of 10.7% compared to 2017, of which more than 2 million Chinese tourists, an increase of 67.2%. Ranked first among Cambodia's 10 priority markets.
By 2020, Cambodia plans to receive more than 7 million international tourists, of which about 3 million will be about 5 billion US dollars, creating millions of direct and indirect jobs.
He also stated that in order to support and supply the growing demand for tourism, Cambodia has been developing itself as a clean and green tourism destination.
At the same time, he said, Cambodia needs investors and development partners to develop new tourism products and infrastructure, strengthen service quality in line with new travel trends, and expand flight connections from Chinese cities to Cambodia. There are currently 636 direct flights a week from China to Cambodia from 58 cities by 25 airlines.
Therefore, by organizing this important cultural and tourism promotion event, it will be a great opportunity for travel agencies and tour operators to start partnering.
Philippine Ambassador: Philippine companies have been investing in mango processing in Prey Veng province for export in the near future.
During the meeting, the Philippine Ambassador expressed his interest in the progress of Cambodia under the leadership of the Royal Government, hoping and believing that the Royal Government of Cambodia can achieve its vision by 2030.
The Ambassador thanked the Minister and his colleagues for their excellent cooperation with the Government of the Philippines through the Embassy in many important matters.
According to the ambassador, with this steady growth, investors from other countries are flocking to invest in Cambodia. Currently, there are Filipino companies that are investing in mango processing in Prey Veng province to export to the Philippine and international markets in the near future.
The ambassador said that in addition, there will be Filipino companies to study the potential of rice in Cambodia in order to invest and process rice to export to the Philippine market. The ambassador said he would help promote direct flights between the two countries in the future to boost tourism and trade.
Mr. Veng Sakhon mentioned the progress made between him and the Ambassador during the previous mission on cooperation between the two countries in the field of agriculture. He said that this cooperation will continue to strengthen and expand, especially to exchange experiences of professional officers related to mango processing technology, rice cultivation techniques and banana processing.
For Cambodian rice products, he asked the government to consider importing Cambodian rice into the Philippine market in the future.
