Restrictions on discrimination on religious beliefs and practices have caused insecurity in some countries, both regionally and globally. For Cambodia, the Constitution states that Buddhism is the state religion, but does not prohibit freedom of religion or belief. In Cambodia today, there are about 95% Buddhists, almost 2% Muslims and about 1.25% Christians, as well as some other religions.
Although Cambodia has a large number of people of different religions, since the country was liberated from the genocidal regime on January 7, 1979, Cambodia has never had any problems caused by these different religions.
Khmer people, Buddhists, Khmer-Muslims and people who believe in Christianity live together in peace. The success of religious harmonization is seen as one of the successes of religious policy led by the Royal Government of Cambodia, headed by Samdech Techo Hun Sen.
Every year, Samdech Techo Hun Sen not only invites to join the "Ramadan" vegetarian meal, but also always invites to eat with Christians. Through this activity, it is shown that Samdech Techo Hun Sen is the leader who has paid the utmost attention to building religious harmony in Cambodia.
It should be noted that from May 7, 2019, corresponding to the second day of Ramadan, 1440, the era of Muhammad, the Muslims of Cambodia, as well as Muslims in the world, are fasting from dawn to dusk for more than 14 hours. Some regional changes last one month a year, which is the fourth of the five obligations of all Muslims in the world.
