Discover 5 Sacred Crops Used in the Thai King's Wedding Ceremony
These five agricultural products are believed by the Thais to be the most powerful and sacred for their nation.
The crown is made of gold mixed with diamonds and other precious stones (emeralds, pomegranates, sapphires, etc.) and has a sacred burial. Measured from the earlobe to the tip of the crown, it is 51 cm long, but the entire crown is 66 cm long and weighs 7.3 kg.
Second. Preah Seng Khad Chey Srey (Praseng Khan Chhay Si): This karma represents the success of defending the kingdom and the people of Thailand. According to the Thai royal family, this kingdom was taken from the Kingdom of Kampuchea during the Odong period during the reign of King Narayan III or King Eng (1772–1796).
This royal kite was caught by a Cambodian fisherman while fishing in the Tonle Sap Lake and handed over to Chao Ponhea Aphei Despas Pen or Apheay Vong (a former Khmer official with the title of Duke Chakrey Pen, but later Surrender to Siam, betrayed the Khmer kingdom) was the governor of Preah Dambang (now Battambang), which at that time Battambang was under Siamese control.
After receiving a beautiful sword from a fisherman and knowing that it was the royal family, Chao Ponhea Aphayvong brought it to the Thai king, Samdech Buddha Yot Phachola Lok or King Ramathibodi I.
Third. Punishment (Than Prakan): is a royal scepter representing the King's honest approval of the Kingdom of Thailand. This scepter is made of gold-plated wood and is 118 cm long.
Fifth. Preah Sovann Sopor Batuka (Chalong Prabat Ngon): A shoe made of pure gold leaf adorned with gems and diamonds. During his reign, a Hindu priest, Baku, presented the golden shoes to the new king on the throne.
