A press release from the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, obtained by Fresh News this evening, states that on April 22, 2019, Prime Minister Samdech Techo sent separate condolences to Vietnamese leaders such as Nguyen. Fu Chung, President of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam; and Nguyen Suon Phuc, Prime Minister of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen expressed deep sorrow over the death of Le Duc Anh, who devoted his entire life to the Vietnamese Revolution and helped liberate Cambodia from the Khmer Rouge genocidal regime. This is a huge loss for the outstanding statesmen of the Vietnamese government and people.
After paying tribute to the late Mr. Le Dek Anh, Samdech Techo Hun Sen and the high-level delegation returned to Cambodia at 11:30 am on May 3, 2019.
On the night of May 2, 2019, Samdech Techo Hun Sen paid a courtesy call on the late Prime Minister Nguyen Tan Dung and paid a working visit to Vietnam.
During the meeting, the Vietnamese Prime Minister, on behalf of the Vietnamese government and people, as well as himself, expressed his deepest respect to Samdech Techo Prime Minister for taking the most valuable time to lead a high-level delegation of the Royal Government and the army of Cambodia pays tribute to the late Le Duc Anh, the outstanding leader of the country and the people of Vietnam.
The Prime Minister thanked the Vietnamese Prime Minister for facilitating the Cambodian delegation to pay tribute to the late Le Duc Anh. Samdech Techo Hun Sen emphasized that Mr. Le Duc Anh was one of the top leaders of Vietnam who had the merit of helping to liberate Cambodia from the genocidal regime of Pol Pot and prevent the return of this brutal regime.
Samdech Techo also said that the presence of Samdech and the delegation to pay their respects to the late Mr. Le Duc Anh is a sign of mutual respect and love as people who used to help each other in difficult times. Each other in the past.
Duc Anh served as President of Vietnam from 1992 to 1997, when in 1995 he became the first Vietnamese head of state to step on American soil after the Vietnam War. Join the 50th anniversary of the United Nations in New York.
