(Beijing): The preservation and adherence to the fine traditions and customs of the nation is a great contribution to the upliftment of the national cultural soul.
As the old saying goes, "culture dies." But those practices that lead to inconsistencies (as conflicts) and burdens on practitioners, we should improve those shortcomings or negatives for the sake of excellence and overall progress.
Note that Cambodia has many traditions, beliefs, religions and festivals that Cambodians practice and celebrate throughout the year. Many of these programs have become a burden for low- and middle-income people. As a burden, some programs have become a social obligation that requires both the celebrant and the participant to fulfill in order to gain social recognition and recognition.
If we think a little more in detail, we will see that they have very little money left to save or reserve for various risks such as health problems, while a small number of Cambodians still have health insurance.
Excessive spending can be considered a serious financial burden for a large number of low-income Cambodians. After marriage, some parents are not able to distribute capital (inheritance) to their children to run a business, but they become debtors and have to try to earn money to pay off their debts. While most of the invited guests are also upset and complain about getting too many invitations each month.
Of course, this is a privilege and can be an honor or a prestige for a lavish ceremony, but running and bragging with a huge focus on material, food, wine and large numbers of guests does not seem to be very profitable. To newlyweds or help promote a few traditions. The important thing is that we celebrate properly according to the traditions and wishes for the newlyweds to live happily and take care of each other for a long time.
