During the visit and visit of the people, Mr. Kouch Chamroeun brought the gift of Samdech Kittipritthbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen to distribute to the victims of two houses that collapsed, one family received some food and 1 million budget Riel for home renovation.
A group of volunteer doctors of the Cambodian-Japanese Young Chamber of Commerce jointly conduct oral examinations and treatment for Cambodian children in remote areas
(Phnom Penh): A group of volunteer doctors from the Cambodian Chamber of Commerce and Industry (JCI) and a group of volunteer doctors from the Japan Chamber of Commerce and Industry (JCI) have joined forces to provide oral examination and treatment services to Cambodian children in remote areas. So far, a team of volunteer doctors from the Cambodian-Japanese Youth Chamber of Commerce has helped at least 1,000 children living in primary schools in remote provinces across Cambodia.
He said that his team had only three members, and that the reason he came to Cambodia was because he saw that children in Cambodia 20 years ago had limited access to health care, and because of the advice of friends. That talks about the situation in Cambodia.
On the other hand, the Cambodian Chamber of Commerce and Industry (JCI) Chaktomuk Branch said that during his volunteer work in Cambodia, he gained a lot of good experience in carrying out humanitarian activities with the team as well as with partner organizations. Particularly relevant is the Ministry of Health in Cambodia.
Dr. Masahiro Iwata from Osaka, who specializes in oral surgery, expressed hope to be able to work with stakeholders and the Cambodian health sector to improve the health sector through both volunteer and participatory activities. Through various missions in needy areas throughout Cambodia.
Dr. Masahiro Iwata told reporters that he had to pay $ 1,500 to $ 1,600 for each trip to Cambodia, including airfare and accommodation. These funds are his own, not sponsored by any party. His participation in Cambodia is to help patients get better health, not to gain any benefits.
