Midwives are also important in ensuring that women can make their own decisions about their sex life and reproductive options, including when and how many children they want and through the provision of care, the ministry said. Qualitative regardless of patient identity or social status.
Midwives have done significant work over the past decade to reduce maternal and infant mortality in Cambodia and have made significant contributions to achieving the fourth and fifth Millennium Development Goals. These achievements can be achieved under the strong leadership and commitment of the Royal Government of Cambodia and investment in health infrastructure and human resources, especially through training, recruitment and retention of midwives.
Professor Mam Bunheng, Minister of Health, said: "We can not achieve these important achievements and save these lives without the participation of witches. On this occasion, I would like to emphasize that we deeply appreciate and value the important work that witchcraft has done and continues to do for the benefit of the future of Cambodia.
Please be informed that Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, signed a sub-decree in 2008 designating May 5 as National Midwifery Day to express gratitude to midwives and to emphasize their important role in Prevention and promotion of health.
The main objectives of this year's National Midwifery Day include:
