In the past, the Donald Trump administration has worked hard to curb the influx of immigrants attempting to enter the southern United States, most of them seeking asylum in his country. In this regard, immigration activists have argued that the US government's actions have had a profound effect on individuals seeking to legally apply for crown rights in order to avoid violence and oppression.
This Monday, Mr. Tram instructed the US Department of Justice and the US Department of Homeland Security to introduce new measures aimed at tightening the asylum policy for 90 days, including setting fees. Asylum applications, as well as barring immigrants to work in the United States until their application is granted.
Under U.S. law, asylum seekers can apply for free, and even if most fail, they can stay and work temporarily in the United States for months or even years. During which their application is subject to review. Mr. Tram also ordered the Ministry of Justice to expedite the proceedings.
