Legal Awareness: Types of Real Estate Rights in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): Real estate refers to land and objects attached to the land and can not be moved, such as buildings, buildings, crops, etc., according to the provisions of Article 120 of the Civil Code of the Kingdom of Cambodia.
For this week's Legal Awareness Program, in collaboration with FRESH NEWS and the Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation, the topic of "Types of Real Estate Rights" will be presented.
For the following interpretation of the above topic, our program only mentions certain types of title deeds of real estate, which refer to land, houses or buildings.
For Cambodia, it is a unique and special case unlike other countries in terms of land management system, both land is state property and private property, since during the Khmer Rouge regime from April 1975 to early 1979, the land management system was Completely eliminated. After the liberation on January 7, 1979, the land management regime was gradually reorganized and evolved from one stage to another.
Prior to 1989, the provision of land for the private sector did not exist, with land being introduced for collective use only, but later in 1989, Cambodia privatized land to provide land ownership and tenure rights. For the people to hold privately.
Then, in 1992, Cambodia enacted a land law, which confirmed the acquisition of ownership by succession, contract, or by changing from "temporary tenure" to "full ownership".
Later, in 2001, another land law was approved by the National Assembly by repealing the 1992 Land Law, which allowed full private ownership of land in Cambodia and established a land registry system for land titles. In Cambodia today.
Through the above evolution, we observe that there are several types of legal documents that define the rights to immovable property, which is land and housing, as part of private property (ownership, possession, use rights ....).
Below are some of the types of documents and title deeds that we often encounter today. What are the legal requirements for this type of document and title deed?
1: Certificate of Identification of Real Estate Owners (Hard copy)
A legal document confirming ownership of immovable property. Ownership theory includes the right to manage, the right to use and the right to enjoy. Every person inscribed on the certificate of ownership of the immovable property (hard copy) is the owner or owner of the immovable property and is protected by law in the exercise of ownership rights and protected by law in all infringement activities. .
The certificate of ownership of the property is registered in the form of a separate registration and a systematic registration. Separate ownership registration is done on request for registration by a legal or legal occupant of the immovable property.
The feature of the property owner identification certificate is to precisely determine the location and size of the property through an ordeal, which is controlled by a computer system with the least possible variability or bias in the future through natural variations.
Soft title deeds are generally seen in many forms, but overall they are a type of document that confirms the actual occupation of a property that does not have a title deed. It may be recognized by the local authority at the commune / sangkat or municipal / district / khan level regarding the possession of the object, but it is not yet fully owned.
Soft title deed is widely used for Cambodian land, which some people find that if the land is soft title deed, the transfer will be easier, faster and less expensive. But at the same time, there can be problems with the uncertainty of management of the property. Land registration in Cambodia has not yet been implemented nationwide, so most of the unregistered land uses this type of title.
The title deed, or Certificate of Ownership of the Private Sector, is a type of title deed that certifies the management of ownership of the co-ownership building. Co-ownership buildings are defined as multi-owner buildings or constructions, some of which are owned separately by each owner (private floor) and some of which are common areas that all owners can use. Yes (common use area).
The purpose of this plan is to facilitate the management of the private sector in a co-ownership building with clarity, as well as to facilitate the transfer of ownership, which generally allows foreigners to become owners.
All in all, the reason why land tenure in Cambodia is used on so many different titles is due to a number of factors such as population needs, political factors, state land management and geographical factors.
Prepared by: Mr. Lim Sovannarong and Mr. Rath Chanthy, Member of the Working Group on Research and Compilation of Legal Articles of the Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation.
Chinese Communist Party Secretary declares that China helps Cambodia in all issues, including EBA
