Ministry of Tourism Holds a Workshop on "Child Safety in Tourism" in Kampong Speu Province
(Kampong Speu): Mr. Hor Sarun, Undersecretary of State of the Ministry of Tourism, represented by Dr. Thong Khon, Minister of Tourism, attended the opening ceremony of the workshop on "Child Safety in Tourism" in Kampong Speu Province on April 25, 2019.
Workshop on "Child Safety in Tourism" organized by the Department of Tourism of Kampong Speu Province in collaboration with the Committee on Child Safety in Tourism, with the participation of the owners or managers of hotels, guesthouses, restaurants, karaoke, youth and 90 related departments.
Mr. Hor Sarun stated that the purpose of organizing today's workshop is to raise awareness and participate in preventing the negative impact of child protection tourism in the tourism industry, build and strengthen the capacity of employers, employees, stakeholders and the private sector in the tourism sector to prevent Sexual exploitation of children in tourism.
Along with the growth of tourism, a number of negative activities have taken place, such as crime, prostitution, human trafficking, child sexual abuse, drug trafficking, etc. The Ministry of Tourism, together with relevant ministries, institutions, the private sector and civil society organizations, have been working closely together to combat these negative activities. Many perpetrators are punished, including foreigners who commit child pornography.
The Royal Government of Cambodia in the sixth legislature remains in a strong position to strengthen anti-trafficking ownership and intensify the prevention and suppression of this crime by not allowing the perpetrators to expand their activities to harm the people. No answer.
Although the perpetrators are more cunning, the authorities have cracked down on almost all cases and continue to investigate new crimes in the country as well as abroad, in close cooperation with the authorities of the countries involved. Without these efforts, human trafficking, exploitation and human abuse would be worse and more unpredictable.
According to the report of the National Committee for Combating Human Trafficking, in 2018, there were 134 cases of human trafficking and sex trafficking, 224 suspects and 230 victims, a decrease of 25 In 159 cases, the number of suspects increased by 21 (203) and the number of victims decreased by 115 (345) compared to 2017.
Once again, I would like to thank Mr. Tith Vuthy, Deputy Governor and Representative of the Provincial Governor for taking the opportunity to attend this opening ceremony. Thank you Mr. Sao Saran, Director of the Provincial Department of Tourism. Child Safety in Tourism who worked hard to organize this course and especially thank you to all of you who participated in today's workshop.
