In just five years, BRI has evolved from a concept to a key platform for building a community with a shared future for humanity and for international public goods.
Cambodia became a member of President Xi Jinping's Belt and Road Initiative in 2016. Both Cambodian officials and experts praised the value of this initiative and provided significant benefits to Cambodia's economic development.
Dr. Kin Phea, President of the Institute of International Relations of the Royal Academy of Cambodia, said that under the Cambodia-China cooperation within the framework of the Belt and Road, China has invested in many major projects in Cambodia, including hydropower, roads, bridges and regional areas. Special economy, Phnom Penh-Sihanoukville Expressway and New Siem Reap International Airport.
Dr. said that Cambodia has high expectations that China will continue to help develop infrastructure in Cambodia. In this way, it will be possible to reduce the price of goods in Cambodia, strengthen competition in economic markets and improve economic growth.
Nak Chendarith, president of the Cambodian Center for the Research of the Silk Road, said: "The infrastructure projects and industrial zones that China has built here will bring a lot of benefits to Cambodia and its people." "These will boost development in Cambodia by helping Cambodia move from a low-middle-income country to a middle-income country by 2030."
Cambodia has seen significant progress in trade and investment relations with China in recent years. This tremendous growth is seen as being linked to the Belt and Road Initiative as well.
In 2017, Chinese investment in Cambodia totaled $ 1,152.82 million, and the investment focused on major sectors such as finance, real estate, factories, hotels and restaurants, agriculture, mining and technology.
In particular, China is also Cambodia's largest donor. In 2018, China recently provided about 740 million US dollars to contribute to the construction and development of Cambodia. In addition, during a meeting between Prime Minister Hun Sen and President Xi Jinping in Beijing in January 2019, the Chinese side pledged about 4 billion yuan (about 587.6 million US dollars) in aid. ) More for the next three years.
Tourism has contributed a lot to job creation and income for the people, especially it contributes about 12.7% of Cambodia's GDP. China has also assisted Cambodia in many social, humanitarian and educational activities.
