Opinions and opinions: In the age of online information, in the age of Facebook and in the age of citizens, there are cameras in almost every hand, doing everything must think about the law!
(Phnom Penh): In the age of technology, the age of information technology is not advanced, public information is easy to be hidden or exaggerated at will, corrupt officials and some powerful people, including crooks, often commit crimes. Injustice in society and on vulnerable citizens at will.
Nowadays, in the age of modern technology, in the age of Facebook, in the age of security cameras, there are almost everywhere, and people have cameras, phones, photos, videos, almost all hands, in the age of free media.
Therefore, the desire to hide information or to exaggerate information by corrupt officials and powerful people, including criminals to commit crimes is rarely hidden from the public eye, especially committing crimes or causing injustice in public. Sooner or later, all crimes or injustices will be made public to law enforcement officials or leaders with specific legal measures.
In fact, in the case of a girl who drove a luxury car and hit and killed a female student in Toul Kork on March 26, 2019, if there were no security cameras to take pictures, injustice could have happened to the victim and her family. But with the help of security cameras, justice was finally found and the perpetrators were brought to justice.
All in all, this is the age of technology, the age of online public information, the age of the streets, the shops, the companies and most of the houses have security cameras, the government leaders have their own Facebook to communicate with the public. Open and free, things can not be hidden or exaggerated at will, sooner or later the truth will be revealed.
Therefore, if they do not want to be in trouble, they will be imprisoned, chained, and exhausted because of their actions, they will continue to commit acts of power, money, and partisanship, they must stop committing crimes, violating the power of their state. Urgent.
By: Neay Khvek
Minister of Women's Affairs: Nearly 80% of women in prison convicted of drug offenses
(Phnom Penh): Ms. Ing Kantha Phavi, Minister of Women's Affairs and Chair of the National Committee for the Promotion of Social Morality, the Values of Women and the Khmer Family, on April 24, 2019, highlighted that there are currently nearly 80 percent of women in prison. Convicted for drug offenses.
This statement was made by Ms. Ing Kantha Phavi at the meeting to review the work to promote social morality, values of women and the Khmer family in 2017-2018 and the direction for 2019-2020, which was held at the Phnom Penh Hotel on the morning of April 24. This year 2019.
Ms. Ing Kantha Phavi stated that "the main problem that women are currently in prison, we have not yet made a clear assessment, but if we look at the situation of women in prison, almost 80 percent. Convicted of drug offenses.
"This is a new issue that we must find ways to reduce, if we talk about eliminating all that is not possible, but at least we must contribute to reduction through education, especially prevention," she said. .
On that occasion, Ms. Ing Kantha Phavi also mentioned that the war in Cambodia, especially the genocidal regime of Pol Pot, has left countless poverty and tragedy on the Cambodian people, especially women as well as children to this day. This includes the analysis of social morality, the values of women and the Cambodian family.
She added that the arrival of globalization has also brought the country progress and many other challenges, including:
* Job competition * Drug addiction, drunkenness, gangsterism * Electronic pornography that causes abuse * Exploitation of all kinds of violence * Human trafficking * Pornography Affect the health, life, social order and put serious pressure on families, communities and society.
She added that the promotion of social morality, the values of Cambodian women and families and the elimination of social inactivity is a complex social education work that cannot be carried out by any separate ministry or institution.
"It means that we need to work together to address the legislature, the executive, the judiciary, development partners, civil society, the private sector, as well as the people." This is the confirmation of Ms. Ing Kantha Phavi.
She stressed that with this, social moral work is a work that involves changing people's attitudes as well as people's mindsets and is a work that can not be done in a hurry and achieve success in the short term.
She said that this work requires time, combined with the common vision of society, as well as high commitment from all walks of life at all levels, by continuing to promote social morality, the values of women and Cambodian families, which are factors contributing to equity, environment, welfare, family and society. World Bank: Cambodia's economy maintains nearly 7% growth in 2019-2021
"After achieving 7.5 percent growth in 2018, Cambodia's economy is expected to turn its attention to long-term potential," the World Bank report said. "Exports are still moderate, while global demand is declining. Real economic growth is estimated to slow slightly to near 7 percent between 2019 and 2021."
However, the long-term outlook depends on the country's ability to absorb the flow of foreign direct investment with the promotion of domestic investment. This is further confirmed in the World Bank report.
