For more information, please visit your nearest Samsung branch and dealer or call: 1800 20 3232. Experts call on government to raise taxes on tobacco products to save lives and improve people's health
(Phnom Penh): Experts from the Southeast Asian Tobacco Control Alliance (SEATCA), the World Health Organization and the Cambodian Movement for Health, have called on the Royal Government of Cambodia to increase tariffs on additional tobacco products to reduce tobacco use and height. Welfare of the people.
Currently, more than 2 million Cambodians use tobacco products, including more than 1.5 million smokers and nearly 0.5 million smokers. Tobacco use among young people is also on the rise. This is according to the joint statement of the experts during the press conference on April 23, 2019 at the Sunway Hotel.
The CEO of SEATCA said that the poor are the most vulnerable to tobacco and tobacco products because cigarettes are cheap or cheap and make it more affordable for them to buy. He does not yet understand the effects of cigarettes and tobacco.
At the same time, Dr. Mom Kong, Executive Director of the Cambodian Movement for Health, stated that "health and economic experts have agreed that we can only benefit public health if taxes on tobacco products are raised. Regularly keep pace with inflation and rising household incomes. "The government should aim to make tobacco products more expensive to reduce consumption."
He said the study showed that people were twice as likely to buy cigarettes compared to 2016 to 2002. A survey of youth opinion conducted by the Royal University of Phnom Penh and the General Department of Taxation of the Ministry of Economy and Finance in 2018 shows that most young people will stop buying cigarettes if the price of cigarettes reaches 10,000 riel per pack. The most popular cigarettes cost only 2,000 riel today.
Tobacco use remains the leading cause of unavoidable death in the world. In Cambodia, about 10,000 people die each year from diseases caused by tobacco use, while the treatment of these diseases costs at least $ 162.70 million a year. Those deaths were from lung cancer, stroke, oral cancer, heart attack, atherosclerosis, stomach cancer, bladder cancer and more.
