April 17 this year marks the 44th anniversary of the Khmer Rouge coming to power
(Phnom Penh): April 17, 2019 is the 44th anniversary of the Khmer Rouge coming to power. 44 years ago, today was the day that the people of Phnom Penh experienced countless chaos and were forcibly evacuated from their homes to the countryside. The prospect of a new regime emerging from the Khmer Rouge revolution that would lead them to peace for the better turned out to be one of the most brutal regimes in the world.
The last battle on April 17, the army of the Khmer Republic raised the white flag and surrendered. The people of the city came out to welcome the arrival of the Khmer Rouge army, dressed in black, armed with 47 AK-47 bombs and grenades. The people are happy and hope that the country is safe and secure because the Khmer Rouge army is under the government headed by Sihanouk.
Since coming to power on April 17, 1975, the Khmer Rouge has continued to call itself the "Royal Government of National Unity of Cambodia", but the real power is not in the hands of this United National Government, but in the hands of the Khmer people. Red alone.
However, what is remarkable is that the Khmer Rouge soldiers in black, mostly children, were between 16 and 17 years old, and they had strong faces and did not smile at all. The soldiers ordered the people to leave the city in two or three days without having to carry much supplies, as they could return home in two or three days.
More than 1.7 million to 2.2 million Cambodians have died as a result of killings, starvation, disease and other inhumane acts.
