(Phnom Penh): A delegation of the Cambodian Youth Party led by Mr. Pich Sros met with Lt. Gen. Run Rath Veasna, Director of the Department of Traffic and Public Order, to find a mechanism to prevent traffic accidents in Cambodia, especially on major national holidays. End in the spirit of working together to prevent traffic accidents in Cambodia.
During the meeting on the morning of April 10, 2019, the two sides agreed on traffic restrictions and the illegal promotion of alcohol.
Separately, Lt. Gen. Run Rath Veasna also asked the party to help speed up the law on alcohol control, which was initiated by the Ministry of Health, this law is still pending. In addition to speeding up the law on alcohol control, he suggested that the Ministry of Education be fully integrated into the traffic curriculum to educate young people about traffic laws from elementary to high school and include traffic lessons in the 12th grade exam. Well.
The Cambodian Youth Party delegation also requested Lt. Gen. Run Rath Veasna to prevent traffic accidents during the upcoming New Year, there are 3 points below:
* First, the government must deploy all kinds of police forces, led by traffic police, to disperse along the main national roads and set a 30-kilometer inspection target for a group of police in order to restrict speeding and control. Excessive alcohol consumption.
* Second: Deploy the authorities in the villages and communes to control people who drink too much alcohol in order to prevent them from using vehicles or traveling. As for the forces, one commune uses 4 to 5 people to patrol to prevent drunk people from driving to the main road.
* Third: The Cambodian government must take the opportunity to disseminate traffic laws and traffic accidents to the Cambodian people during the Khmer New Year by establishing a team to educate the locals and the main roads that Cambodians travel to visit. Or entertainment venue.
Mr. Pich Sros confirmed that for the above three points, the side of Lt. Gen. Run Rath Veasna also accepted and decided to test during the upcoming Khmer New Year to protect the lives of Cambodians.
