Senior Minister Yim Nola urges leaders of the Economic, Social and Cultural Council to work more actively
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Yim Nola, Senior Minister in charge of Special Mission, Chairman of the Economic, Social and Cultural Council, on April 10, 2019, requested the leadership, members and officials of the General Secretariat of the Economic, Social and Cultural Council (ECOSOCC). All must work harder to be more active.
Senior Minister Yim Nola made the request on the occasion of presiding over a workshop on disseminating the 10-year achievements of the Economic, Social and Cultural Council and the implementation of a legal impact assessment program. This morning, April 10, 2019.
"Following the Rectangular Strategy Phase 4 and following Samdech Techo's recommendations, I urge all leaders, members and officials of the General Secretariat (ECOSOCC) to work more actively," said Senior Minister Yim Nola.
He added that in particular, strengthening the capacity of knowledge in the fields of economy, society, culture, trade, politics, diplomacy, both regionally and globally.
Mr. Yim Nola also hoped that all ECOSOCC members would use all of their intelligence to contribute to the full range of opinions and analysis on draft laws and regulations, as well as other policy documents. .
According to Senior Minister Yim Nola, the actual achievements over the past 10 years by the Economic, Social and Cultural Council include:
First: Monthly, quarterly, semi-annual and annual analysis of the socio-economic and cultural situation on consumer price and exchange rate situation, budget situation, monetary and interest rate situation, agricultural situation, industry, energy, services and culture and trade balance situation. The meeting was attended by representatives of relevant ministries and institutions.
Second: Research on topics that are important challenges related to the economic, social and cultural sectors that influence the development of the country and the daily lives of the people.
Third: Participated in reviewing and commenting on the draft legal documents
Fifth, completed other additional tasks assigned by the Royal Government, such as some infrastructure projects.
